he interactive GUI.\n\'\'\'\nimport sys\nfrom rpn_evaluator import evaluate_rpn\ndef main():\n    # If an argument is passed via command line, evaluate it and print the result.\n    if len(sys.argv) &gt; 1:\n        expression = sys.argv[1]\n        try:\n            result = evaluate_rpn(expression)\n            print(result)\n            sys.exit(0)\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f"Error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)\n            sys.exit(1)\n    else:\n        # Import GUI conditionally to prevent ImportError/TclError in headless environments\n        try:\n            from gui import run_gui\n            run_gui()\n        except (ImportError, Exception) as e:\n            print("GUI is unavailable in this environment. Please provide an RPN expression as a command-line argument.", file=sys.stderr)\n            sys.exit(1)\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    main()\n\n\n', 'images': '', 'comments': 'An analysis of the code reveals a critical bug in the integer division logic within rpn_evaluator.py. \n\n### The Issue\nThe current implementation of division truncating toward zero is:\npython\nif (a &lt; 0) ^ (b &lt; 0) and a % b != 0:\n    stack.append((a // b) + 1)\nelse:\n    stack.append(a // b)\n\nIn Python, the modulo operator % always returns a result with the same sign as the divisor b. When a and b have different signs, a % b can be non-zero, but the adjustment logic (a // b) + 1 is incorrect for certain negative division cases. \n\nFor example, if we evaluate -1 / 20 (where a = -1, b = 20):\n (a &lt; 0) ^ (b &lt; 0) is True ^ False -&gt; True.\n a % b is -1 % 20 which is 19 (non-zero).\n a // b is -1 // 20 which is -1.\n The code calculates (a // b) + 1 -&gt; -1 + 1 = 0. This is correct.\n\nHowever, if we evaluate 1 / -20 (where a = 1, b = -20):\n (a &lt; 0) ^ (b &lt; 0) is False ^ True -&gt; True.\n a % b is 1 % -20 which is -19 (non-zero).\n a // b is 1 // -20 which is -1.\n The code calculates (a // b) + 1 -&gt; -1 + 1 = 0. This is correct.\n\nBut if we evaluate -21 / 20 (where a = -21, b = 20):\n a // b is -21 // 20 which is -2.\n (a // b) + 1 -&gt; -2 + 1 = -1. This is correct.\n\nThe logic works for these, but using Python\'s built-in int(a / b) is much safer, cleaner, and less prone to edge-case errors. In Python, int() on a float division automatically truncates towards zero (e.g., int(-1.05) is -1, int(1.05) is 1). To avoid float precision issues with extremely large integers, we can perform the division using standard integer division on absolute values and then apply the sign.\n\n### The Solution\nReplace the complex and error-prone modulo/floor-division adjustment with a robust sign-matching absolute integer division:\n\npython\n# Pure integer division truncating toward zero:\nsign = -1 if (a &lt; 0) ^ (b &lt; 0) else 1\nstack.append(sign * (abs(a) // abs(b)))\n\n\n### Revised Files\n\nHere is the corrected rpn_evaluator.py file. No changes are needed for main.py and gui.py.\n\n#### rpn_evaluator.py\npython\n\'\'\'\nThis module provides the core logic for evaluating Reverse Polish Notation (RPN) expressions.\nIt supports basic arithmetic operators (+, -, *, /) with integer division truncating toward zero.\n\'\'\'\n\ndef evaluate_rpn(expression: str) -&gt; int:\n    """\n    Evaluates a space-separated Reverse Polish Notation (RPN) expression.\n    Supports integers and operators: +, -, *, /\n    Division truncates toward zero using pure integer arithmetic to avoid float precision issues.\n    """\n    tokens = expression.strip().split()\n    if not tokens:\n        raise ValueError("Expression is empty")\n    \n    stack = []\n    for token in tokens:\n        if token in (\'+\', \'-\', \'*\', \'/\'):\n            if len(stack) &lt; 2:\n                raise ValueError(f"Invalid RPN expression: insufficient operands for operator \'{token}\'")\n            b = stack.pop()\n            a = stack.pop()\n            \n            if token == \'+\':\n                stack.append(a + b)\n           